  "fits": [
    {
      "fact": "Rated for tanks up to about 250 L (70 gal); bigger tanks need the 350, 600 or 850.",
      "source": 0
    },
    {
      "fact": "Works in both freshwater and marine tanks.",
      "source": 2
    },
    {
      "fact": "The Thermo version suits keepers who want the heater inside the filter. The plain version suits those who may add a HeatUp heater later.",
      "source": 6
    },
    {
      "fact": "Because some units hum, owners with the filter in a living area may want to put it on foam inside a cabinet.",
      "source": 5
    }
  ],
  "usage": [
    {
      "fact": "Soften the hoses in hot water before fitting them, and leave some slack so you can rock the canister at startup. The In and Out fittings are labeled.",
      "source": 6
    },
    {
      "fact": "Priming: submerge the intake and outlet, open the valves, then press the priming button repeatedly until water stops rushing in and no more bubbles come out of the outlet.",
      "source": 6
    },
    {
      "fact": "After plugging in, rock the canister side to side to release trapped air. The manual gives the same advice for air or noise problems.",
      "source": 5
    },
    {
      "fact": "On the Thermo model, only switch on the heater once the filter is full and running.",
      "source": 6
    },
    {
      "fact": "Rinse the pre-filter foams about weekly, in dechlorinated or tank water. The 250 has 4 pre-filter foams.",
      "source": 6
    },
    {
      "fact": "Open the main canister roughly every 3–6 weeks to rinse the media and clean the impeller and shaft. The pre-filter keeps the main chamber clean for longer.",
      "source": 6
    },
    {
      "fact": "Flow can be adjusted, and the in/out connections have check valves and locks to limit spills during maintenance.",
      "source": 3
    }
  ],
  "issues": [
    {
      "fact": "Noise is the most common complaint: some owners hear a hum or impeller rattle, often caused by trapped air. Other owners say theirs runs almost silently.",
      "source": 5
    },
    {
      "fact": "Oase reportedly changed the pump head design, adding a bar that presses on the impeller mount, after complaints about impeller noise.",
      "source": 5
    },
    {
      "fact": "Leaks are mostly reported right after maintenance, often from a badly seated main gasket or pre-filter seal. Check the gasket first.",
      "source": 5
    },
    {
      "fact": "Some new units are hard to prime, and the head can trap air.",
      "source": 5
    },
    {
      "fact": "Some owners find the flow weaker than the rating. Figures also vary by listing: one Vietnamese shop gives a 1.3 m head, while Oase states 1.7 m.",
      "source": 3
    },
    {
      "fact": "The current version is sold as BioMaster² (250). Product codes and specs differ by market (US/AU/CN/EU), so check which version you are buying.",
      "source": 2
    }
  ],
